#{[17592186248465 :answer/value "4cos⁡x+3{csc}^{2}⁡x+C"] [17592186248585 :answer/value "-6sin⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186248696 :answer/value "6sin⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186248750 :answer/value "-5cos⁡y+C"] [17592186248842 :answer/value "-4cos⁡x+3csc⁡x+C"] [17592186248866 :answer/value "-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186248893 :answer/value "3sec⁡x+C"] [17592186248923 :answer/value "-6cos⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186248966 :answer/value "-3sec⁡x+C"] [17592186248982 :answer/value "-5tan⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186249020 :answer/value "2cos⁡x-3sin⁡x+C"] [17592186249051 :answer/value "4cos⁡x+3sec⁡x+C"] [17592186249161 :answer/value "-4cos⁡x-3cot⁡x+C"] [17592186249217 :answer/value "5cot⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186249233 :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186249513 :answer/value "2tan⁡\\theta +C"] [17592186249539 :answer/value "3cos⁡x-2sin⁡x+C"] [17592186249596 :answer/value "3sin⁡x-2cos⁡x+C"] [17592186265711 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186265711 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(0,-1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(0,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{0+1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265712 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.$"] [17592186265712 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(1)-2(-2)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265713 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186265713 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+1(2)}{(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{(x+y)}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y} & =\\frac{2}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"] [17592186265714 :question/problem "Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"]}
